# Session-start briefing SOP

Before taking any repo action in a new session — Cursor, Codex, Claude Code,
or Copilot — read the most recent file in `research/daily-briefs/` (sort by
filename date, open the latest `YYYY-MM-DD.md`).

## Why

Cursor, Codex, Claude Code, and Copilot all work this repo, sometimes
concurrently, with no live channel between them. The daily brief is the
durable, git-versioned handoff point — commits/MRs merged, coherence queue
movement, milestone movement, guard status — produced by
`@aea-coherence-guardian`. Reading it first is cheaper and more reliable
than re-deriving the same state from `git log` / `glab` from scratch every
session, and it lowers the odds of branching from a stale understanding of
`main` (this happened at least once on this repo: a session branched a
coherence finding from a `main` that had already advanced past what it had
fetched).

## What "read first" means

1. `ls research/daily-briefs/*.md`, sort by date, open the most recent.
2. If today's brief does not exist, read the most recent one available and
   treat anything after its date as **unknown** — verify against `git log`
   / `glab` directly for that gap rather than assuming nothing happened
   since. A brief more than ~1 day old is stale; say so if you're relying
   on one that old.
3. **Verify the brief you're reading is actually committed**, not a local
   file: `git log --oneline -- research/daily-briefs/<file>`. This is not
   paranoia — it happened. Four briefs (2026-08-11, 2026-08-12, 2026-08-14,
   2026-08-18) were written to disk across multiple sessions and never
   `git add`ed, so a fresh clone or a different machine had zero visibility
   into any of them despite this SOP telling every session to read them. A
   brief that exists only in one local working tree is not shared memory —
   treat an uncommitted brief the same as a missing one for the purpose of
   this SOP, and separately flag it as an incident (someone ran
   `@aea-coherence-guardian` and didn't finish the job).
3. The brief is an onboarding accelerant, not a source of truth for
   right-now. Still verify live state yourself before an irreversible
   action (merge, `terraform apply`, force-push) — do not skip that
   verification because the brief said something was fine as of its date.

## Who keeps it fresh

`@aea-coherence-guardian` owns writing `research/daily-briefs/YYYY-MM-DD.md`
(`.cursor/skills/aea-coherence-guardian/SKILL.md`). If the latest brief is
stale when a session starts, either invoke `@aea-coherence-guardian` to
produce a fresh one, or explicitly note in your own work that you're
operating without current cross-session state. Do not silently proceed as
if a stale brief were current.

## Enforcement across tools

Each tool's always-loaded context file points here rather than restating
this procedure:

- Claude Code: `CLAUDE.md` → "Session start"
- Cursor: this rule (`alwaysApply: true`)
- Codex: `AGENTS.md` → "Session start"
- Copilot: `.github/copilot-instructions.md` → "Session start"

Do not duplicate this procedure in each of those files — point to this one,
so a change here does not require four edits to stay consistent.
